      Security Fence Mesh: Anti-Climb Specs for Facilities and Perimeters
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 11 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> A fence is "anti-climb" when the openings are too small to grip and too tight to cut quickly: welded mesh with openings no larger than about 300 × 75 mm for general facilities, or 76.2 × 12.7 mm "358" prison mesh for high-security sites, with wire at least 3.0–4.0 mm (8 gauge for 358) and a 60 × 60 × 2.5 mm minimum RHS post at 2.5–3.0 m centers set in concrete. Add toppings—barbed or razor wire at 45° outriggers, or an electric alarm wire—for anything above basic deterrence. Verify weld strength to ASTM F2453, posts and coatings to ASTM F1043/F668, and budget $45–90 per meter installed for welded mesh systems and $120–220 per meter for genuine 358 systems, excluding toppings and civil works.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
