    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Specifying a security perimeter is a delay game: every extra minute a fence takes to defeat buys response time. Procurement engineers get into trouble when they buy on price per panel without checking opening geometry, weld integrity, and post section—three places where cheap security fence quietly fails. This guide gives the numeric specs behind "anti-climb" claims so your RFQ can demand them. See our <a href="/applications/fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">fence applications page</a> for the full product range across site and perimeter use.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Anti-climb -->
    <section id="anti-climb" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">What Makes a Fence "Anti-Climb"</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Three geometry-based criteria separate anti-climb mesh from ordinary fencing. First, <strong>opening size</strong>: finger and toe holds need roughly 75 mm or more of clear width and height; mesh at or below 300 × 75 mm removes easy grips, and 358 mesh (76.2 × 12.7 mm) removes them entirely. Second, <strong>wire diameter</strong>: under about 3 mm, bolt cutters pass through a wire in one bite; 4 mm wire with small apertures forces slow, noisy cutting that scales badly over a long perimeter. Third, <strong>rigidity</strong>: welded mesh on a framed panel with closely spaced posts resists racking and crawling-under far better than flexible fabric like chain link. Anti-climb is therefore a system property—mesh, frame, posts, and footings—not a mesh property alone.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Opening ≤ 300 × 75 mm:</strong> standard anti-climb threshold for commercial and industrial sites.</li>
        <li><strong>Opening ≤ 76 × 13 mm (358):</strong> high-security threshold; no viable finger holds, nearly impossible to cut without power tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Wire ≥ 3.0 mm general / 4.0 mm high security:</strong> raises cutting time per panel from seconds to minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Panel height ≥ 2.4 m</strong> plus topping reach, per most corporate security standards and government facility guidance.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Mesh types -->
    <section id="mesh-types" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Mesh Types: 358, 3510, and Welded Panel Grids</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The numbers in 358 and 3510 are imperial shorthand: <strong>3 inches × 0.5 inch mesh from 8 gauge wire</strong> (76.2 × 12.7 mm openings, 4.0 mm wire) for 358, and <strong>3 inches × 1 inch from 10 gauge</strong> (76.2 × 25.4 mm, 3.0 mm wire) for the lighter 3510. Welded panel grids—the <a href="/products/wire-mesh-fence-panels/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">wire mesh fence panels</a> used on most industrial sites—typically run 200 × 50 mm or 300 × 75–100 mm openings in 4–5 mm wire.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">System</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Opening (mm)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Wire (mm)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Security Level</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Use</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">358 prison mesh</td>
              <td class="p-3">76.2 × 12.7</td>
              <td class="p-3">4.0</td>
              <td class="p-3">High</td>
              <td class="p-3">Prisons, data centers, airports, military</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">3510 security mesh</td>
              <td class="p-3">76.2 × 25.4</td>
              <td class="p-3">3.0</td>
              <td class="p-3">Medium-high</td>
              <td class="p-3">Warehouses, utilities, schools</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Welded panel 200 × 50 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">200 × 50</td>
              <td class="p-3">4.0–5.0</td>
              <td class="p-3">Medium</td>
              <td class="p-3">Factories, logistics yards, parks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Chain link 50 × 50 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">50 × 50</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.5–3.5 woven</td>
              <td class="p-3">Low-medium</td>
              <td class="p-3">Temporary sites, low-risk boundaries</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Chain link belongs at the perimeter only where the asset risk is low—its woven structure stretches, climbs easily, and cuts fast. Where it is acceptable, see <a href="/products/chain-link-fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">chain link fence options</a> and the <a href="/blog/chain-link-vs-welded-fence-panels/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">chain link vs welded panel cost comparison</a> before deciding. For anything designated "security" in a risk assessment, specify welded panels as the floor and 358 for high-value or regulated sites.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Posts -->
    <section id="posts" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Wire Diameter, Frames, and Post Specs</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The post line carries wind load, impact, and the racking force of someone shaking a panel. Under-specified posts are the single most common defect in imported fence systems:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Element</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">General Site Security</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">High Security (358)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Post section</td>
              <td class="p-3">60 × 60 × 2.5 mm RHS</td>
              <td class="p-3">80 × 80 × 3.0 mm RHS (or 101 mm round, 3 mm wall)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Post spacing</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.5–3.0 m</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.5–3.0 m, with clamp bar every panel</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Post embedment</td>
              <td class="p-3">450–600 mm in C25 concrete, 300 mm cube</td>
              <td class="p-3">600–750 mm in C30 concrete</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Panel frame</td>
              <td class="p-3">Optional; folded mesh edge acceptable</td>
              <td class="p-3">40 × 40 × 3.0 mm RHS frame, mesh welded to frame</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Fixing</td>
              <td class="p-3">Anti-tamper bolts or security clamps</td>
              <td class="p-3">Security clamps with shear-nut fixings, inside face</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Finish</td>
              <td class="p-3">Hot-dip galvanized ≥ 42 µm zinc (ASTM A123 class) + polyester powder coat optional</td>
              <td class="p-3">Galvanized ≥ 55 µm + powder coat ≥ 80 µm total</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Fixings belong on the <strong>protected (inside) face</strong> so they cannot be reached from outside the perimeter. For coastal or chemically aggressive sites, move up to Galfan (95% Zn / 5% Al) coating or 316-grade stainless fixings—standard zinc coatings pit within a few years in marine air.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Toppings -->
    <section id="toppings" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Toppings: Barbed Wire, Razor Wire, and Detection</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Three-strand barbed wire</strong> on 45° outriggers: the baseline for commercial sites; adds roughly 300 mm of effective height; cheap but defeatable with a blanket or cutters.</li>
        <li><strong>Concertina / razor wire (BTO-22, CBT-65):</strong> flat wrap or coil on outriggers or as a topping along the fence crown; the standard for 358 installations, detention facilities, and utilities. CBT-65 with its denser clip pattern is harder to crush down than BTO-22.</li>
        <li><strong>Flat-profile razor mesh:</strong> welded razor mesh panels topping a 358 fence give the anti-climb property to the top of the barrier as well.</li>
        <li><strong>Detection:</strong> fiber-optic or microphonic cable on the mesh, or fence-mounted PIR zones, converts delay into alarm response. Detection matters more than an extra meter of height for most corporate sites.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Budget impact is modest relative to the fence itself: barbed-wire outriggers add $6–12 per meter; concertina toppings add $15–40 per meter depending on coil diameter and profile.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Standards -->
    <section id="standards" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Standards and Test Requirements</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Name standards on the purchase order so quality is testable rather than promised:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>ASTM F2453 / F2453M:</strong> welded wire fence fabric—the governing standard for 358-style security mesh; covers wire tensile, weld shear strength, and dimensional tolerances.</li>
        <li><strong>ASTM F1043:</strong> strength and protective coating requirements for fence framework (posts, rails, gates).</li>
        <li><strong>ASTM F668 / F2349:</strong> polymer (powder/PVC) coatings on steel fence components—specify the Type and coating mass, not just "powder coated."</li>
        <li><strong>ASTM A123 / A153:</strong> hot-dip galvanizing of structural steel and hardware, with minimum coating thickness by material thickness.</li>
        <li><strong>LPS 1175 (UK) and ISO 18000-class forced-entry test regimes:</strong> where a certified resistance rating (e.g., SR1–SR3, A1–D10) is contractually required, only buy from factories with tested and certified systems—self-declared "prison grade" mesh is common and meaningless without a certificate.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        At pre-shipment inspection, verify three things that cheap imports most often miss: <strong>actual wire diameter</strong> (micrometer, 4.0 mm ±0.1 mm for 358), <strong>weld shear strength</strong> per F2453 on sampled intersections, and <strong>zinc coating thickness</strong> with a magnetic gauge at multiple points per panel.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Cost -->
    <section id="cost" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Installed Cost per Meter</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Imported systems cut material cost dramatically—Chinese FOB prices for security mesh panels typically run $25–45 per m² for 358 mesh and $12–22 per m² for welded panels—but installation labor, posts, and civil works dominate on most projects:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">System (2.4 m height, installed)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Materials (USD/m)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Labor + Civils (USD/m)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Total Installed (USD/m)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Chain link, 3.0 mm wire, barbed top</td>
              <td class="p-3">10–18</td>
              <td class="p-3">15–25</td>
              <td class="p-3">25–43</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Welded panel mesh, 200 × 50, 4 mm wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">18–32</td>
              <td class="p-3">25–45</td>
              <td class="p-3">45–90</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">3510 security mesh, framed</td>
              <td class="p-3">35–55</td>
              <td class="p-3">35–55</td>
              <td class="p-3">75–120</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">358 prison mesh, framed + concertina top</td>
              <td class="p-3">60–100</td>
              <td class="p-3">45–90</td>
              <td class="p-3">120–220</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Gates, turnstiles, vehicle barriers, and detection systems are quoted separately and can add 20–50% to a perimeter budget. When comparing import quotes, strip freight and local charges out first with the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a>—a "cheap" 358 panel loses its advantage fast if the quote hides $40/m² of local cost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What does "358 fence" mean?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            It is imperial shorthand for the mesh geometry: 3 inches × 0.5 inch openings (76.2 × 12.7 mm) from 8 gauge wire (4.0 mm). The dense, finger-proof grid is nearly impossible to climb or cut quickly without power tools, which is why 358 is the default for prisons, airports, and data centers. The lighter 3510 version uses 3 × 1 inch openings in 10 gauge (3.0 mm) wire.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What opening size counts as anti-climb?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Openings at or below about 300 × 75 mm remove practical finger and toe holds for general commercial security. For high-security sites, 76 × 13 mm (358) is the accepted threshold—no usable holds at all. Height matters too: a 2.4 m panel plus a topping outrigger puts the effective barrier near 3 m, which measurably deters casual intrusion.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What post size do I need for security fencing?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For welded panel systems: 60 × 60 × 2.5 mm RHS posts at 2.5–3.0 m centers, embedded 450–600 mm in concrete. For 358 high-security systems: 80 × 80 × 3.0 mm RHS (or 101 mm round, 3 mm wall) at 2.5–3.0 m centers, embedded 600–750 mm in C30 concrete, with clamp bars securing every panel. Posts carry wind and racking loads; undersized posts make even good mesh fail.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which standards should I name when ordering security fence mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            ASTM F2453 for welded wire fence fabric (wire tensile and weld shear), ASTM F1043 for framework strength and coating, ASTM F668 or F2349 for polymer coating mass, and ASTM A123 for galvanizing thickness. If a certified forced-entry rating (LPS 1175 SR1–SR3 or equivalent) is required, demand the factory's test certificates—"prison grade" claims without certification are common on imported mesh.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is chain link acceptable for security perimeters?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Only for low-risk boundaries and temporary sites. Woven chain link flexes, offers 50 mm footholds every 50 mm of height, and cuts in seconds with small tools. For any asset where the risk assessment uses the word "security," specify welded mesh panels minimum; see the <a href="/blog/chain-link-vs-welded-fence-panels/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">chain link vs welded panel comparison</a> for lifespan and cost numbers.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

